{
  "image_name": "0b7e50b3079da67cc0558eb841c82d81.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how oncogenic EGFR signaling shapes the tumor immune microenvironment and how EGFR inhibition can reverse several suppressive effects. In the tumor cell, an activating EGFR alteration drives multiple downstream pathways, including RAS, JAK2, PI3K, NF-κB, and IRF1, leading to secretion of chemokines and cytokines such as CCL22, CXCL10, IL-6, IL-8, IL-10, and VEGF, as well as EGFR ligands. These outputs influence surrounding immune cells and collectively promote a pro-tumoral inflammatory state marked by immunosuppressive and tumor-supporting factors.\n\nThe diagram shows several key immune consequences: recruitment of Treg cells, suppression of CD8+ T cells, altered NK-cell interactions through NKG2D ligands, inhibition of dendritic-cell activity, and polarization of macrophages toward an M2-like tumor-associated phenotype rather than an M1-like state. Myeloid cells and Treg cells are also depicted as responding to receptor signaling through pathways that enhance IL-6, NF-κB activity, or FOXP3 stability, reinforcing immune suppression.\n\nBoxes labeled “EGFR signalling inhibition” indicate that blocking EGFR appears to reduce these suppressive circuits, promote dendritic-cell and CD8+ T-cell activity, lower Treg influence, and shift macrophages away from the M2 phenotype. The lower right also highlights adenosine production via CD39/CD73 as another immunosuppressive mechanism in the tumor milieu.",
